.TH SSE_INIT 3
.SH NAME
sse_init, sse_free, sse_feed \- parse a server-sent events stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sse.h>
.P
.BI "void sse_init(sse* " s ");"
.br
.BI "void sse_free(sse* " s ");"
.br
.BI "size_t sse_feed(sse* " s ", const char* " x ", size_t " n ", sse_callback* " cb ", void* " user ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
A server-sent events stream (media type text/event-stream) is what the APIs
of language models answer with when asked to stream. It is made of lines
such as
.B data: {...}
and an empty line ends each event.
.P
.BR sse_init ()
prepares the parser
.IR s ,
.BR sse_free ()
releases what it holds.
.BR sse_feed ()
parses the
.I n
bytes at
.I x
and calls
.I cb
with every event they complete. The bytes may be cut anywhere, also in the
middle of a line or between the CR and the LF of a line end, and the next
call goes on from there. Lines may end in LF, CR LF or CR. Comment lines
and fields other than data, event, id and retry are ignored.
.P
The event given to the callback is valid until the callback returns.
It has the event type (message if there is none), the data with its lines
joined by newlines, the last id and the retry time in milliseconds or -1.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR sse_feed ()
returns the number of events it dispatched.
.SH SEE ALSO
http_post(3)
